Greetings;

lbalance is a match target intended for load balancing that uses current 
bandwidth usage (as opposed to randomicity, or round-robin rotation, or 
number of NAT connections) as the underlying choice criterion. Assuming 
that eth0 has twice as much bandwidth as eth1:

# Measure downstream bandwidth by accumulating statistics into slots 0 
and 1:
iptables -t mangle -A PREROUTING -i eth0 -m lbalance --record 0 --weight 
128
iptables -t mangle -A PREROUTING -i eth1 -m lbalance --record 1 --weight 
64

# Choose the route with the lowest current usage:
... -m lbalance --test 0 -j ... # slot 0 has the lowest usage, therefore 
pick eth0
... -m lbalance --test 1 -j ... # likewise for eth1

This example measures downstream bandwidth because the first two rules 
are placed in mangle/PREROUTING. Upstream bandwidth or joint bandwidth 
could be measured instead by placing the rules elsewhere. Current 
statistics are gathered in /proc/net/ipt_lbalance:

host# cat /proc/net/ipt_lbalance
0 153921
1 140291
host#

These values are exponentially decayed byte counts with a 1-second 
half-life (i.e. roughly equivalent to instantaneous Bps.)

Please let me know if this builds/works for you.

Regards,
Luca Raggi
